SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNICEF: Children call for more government investment in education, health and protection","publisher":"UNICEF Bangladesh","publication_date":"2022-06-21","url":"https://www.unicef.org/bangladesh/en/press-releases/unicef-children-call-more-government-investment-education-health-and-protection","claim":"Nine in ten Bangladeshi children said it was very important for government to spend more on education and health; over 85 percent prioritized more education spending to recover learning loss; over 65 percent prioritized more social workers; UNICEF also said child-sector investment had stagnated at around 15 percent of public expenditure.","locator":"lines 67-84","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct UNICEF press release with concrete survey findings and budget-related claims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 budget and child-welfare priorities","ruler_attribution":"Indirect. This reflects government policy priorities under the Hasina administration, not a personal statement by Sheikh Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["This is a survey of preferences and budget concerns, so it does not prove that policy delivered the desired outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Government commits to strengthening the social services workforce for the protection of children","publisher":"UNICEF Bangladesh","publication_date":"2022-09-19","url":"https://www.unicef.org/bangladesh/en/press-releases/government-commits-strengthening-social-services-workforce-protection-children","claim":"The Government of Bangladesh said it would recruit 6,000 new social workers, increasing the workforce from 3,000 to 9,000, and the symposium was opened by Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina, who said child protection services should be available at community level.","locator":"lines 65-67","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UNICEF report of a government commitment, with direct quote from the Prime Minister.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 institutional expansion","ruler_attribution":"Direct enough for attribution of government commitment; the source does not prove that Hasina personally designed the staffing plan, but it does place the commitment in her public presence and message.","contrary_evidence":["The same page says 45 million children under 15 were regularly subjected to physical and psychological violence at home and that Bangladesh had only 3,000 social workers before the expansion."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Learning from the education sector's COVID-19 response to prepare for future emergencies (Bangladesh)","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.unicef.org/documents/learning-education-sectors-covid-19-response-prepare-future-emergencies-bangladesh","claim":"Bangladesh's 20-month COVID-19 school closures led the Ministry of Education to create a remote learning strategy using television, radio, and digital lessons, plus printed materials that supported 150,933 children from marginalized groups in 842 schools.","locator":"About section, lines 193-195","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF publication with specific implementation detail and target-group coverage numbers.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"retrospective on the 2022 education recovery context","ruler_attribution":"Indirect. This is a ministry-led response under the Hasina administration rather than a personal directive by Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["The strategy was a response to long school closures, so it shows remediation of damage as much as proactive gain."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WHO Bangladesh Country Cooperation Strategy: 2020-2025","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2023-01-11","url":"https://www.who.int/southeastasia/publications/i/item/9789290209478","claim":"WHO said Bangladesh had made remarkable socioeconomic progress, including poverty reduction, improvements in women's education, rising life expectancy, and better population health indicators such as under-five mortality reduction, while also noting progress in reducing maternal, infant, and child deaths, malnutrition, and increasing immunization coverage; the government prioritized people-centred accessible health systems focused on governance, health-system strengthening, and quality.","locator":"overview lines 170-176","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary WHO country strategy summarizing progress and government priorities in health systems.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 policy and health-system context","ruler_attribution":"Government-level health policy under the Hasina administration, not a personal ruling act.","contrary_evidence":["WHO also says the system still faced high out-of-pocket payments, inequitable access, weak quality of care, shortages of skilled providers, and widespread informal providers."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh country strategic plan (2022-2026)","publisher":"World Food Programme","publication_date":"2021-11","url":"https://www.wfp.org/operations/bd02-bangladesh-country-strategic-plan-2022-2026","claim":"WFP said Bangladesh was self-sufficient in rice, meat, and fish and on track to meet high-level targets for reduced stunting and wasting, but still faced a triple burden of malnutrition; its strategic outcomes aimed to ensure basic food and nutrition needs during crises and meet the nutrition needs of women, children, and vulnerable groups through strengthened national institutions and gender- and nutrition-sensitive social safety nets.","locator":"lines 264-275","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary WFP strategy document with explicit country objectives and vulnerability focus.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 strategy window","ruler_attribution":"Indirect. This is the government-aligned WFP strategy environment rather than a personal Hasina statement.","contrary_evidence":["The same plan says the pandemic created a new group of poor and vulnerable Bangladeshis, especially in urban areas, and that Rohingya refugees remained highly food-insecure without assistance."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WFP Bangladesh - Assessments","publisher":"World Food Programme","publication_date":"2022-08-30","url":"https://www.wfp.org/publications/wfp-bangladesh-assessments","claim":"WFP said nearly one third of Bangladesh's population faced food insecurity and 20 percent lived below the national poverty line; since 2017 it had been providing food assistance and nutrition services in the Cox's Bazar Rohingya camps and surrounding community, along with life-skills training, disaster-risk-reduction activities, and engineering services.","locator":"lines 260-261","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary WFP overview page summarizing the country's vulnerability and program response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 vulnerability and humanitarian response","ruler_attribution":"Country-level context under the Hasina administration; the source does not attribute the conditions to one person specifically.","contrary_evidence":["The page emphasizes significant economic growth, so the food-insecurity figures do not imply total policy failure but they do show major unmet need."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Over 1.5 million children at risk as devastating floods hit Bangladesh","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-05-23","url":"https://www.unicef.org/press-releases/over-15-million-children-risk-devastating-floods-hit-bangladesh-unicef","claim":"UNICEF said more than 1.5 million children were at increased risk from flooding in north-eastern Bangladesh and that it was on the ground with government and non-government partners to meet urgent safety, health, nutrition, clean-water, and education needs; UNICEF was also supporting the government flood response with water purification tablets, hygiene kits, therapeutic milk, learning kits, and social workers.","locator":"lines 194-200","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UNICEF emergency response statement with concrete numbers and response actions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 crisis response","ruler_attribution":"Indirect government response context under the Hasina administration, not a personal act by Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["The same report shows the scale of harm: over four million people were affected, schools were submerged, and children were losing learning time."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Structural Reforms are Essential to Sustain Growth & Poverty Reduction in Bangladesh","publisher":"World Bank Group","publication_date":"2023-10-05","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2023/10/05/structural-reforms-are-essential-to-sustain-growth-poverty-reduction-in-bangladesh","claim":"The World Bank said extreme poverty in Bangladesh fell to 5 percent in 2022 from 9 percent in 2016, and that other wellbeing measures also improved, including infant mortality, stunting, access to electricity, sanitary toilets, and education; rural areas saw faster poverty reduction than urban areas, though urban inequality widened.","locator":"story highlights and lines 35-37","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank retrospective on 2022 poverty and wellbeing outcomes with explicit metrics.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"retrospective on 2022 outcomes","ruler_attribution":"Outcome-level evidence under the Hasina administration, not direct proof of a specific personal decision.","contrary_evidence":["The same article says sustaining growth requires policy adjustments and notes balance-of-payments pressure, rising inflation, and financial-sector vulnerabilities."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Public Finance for Children","publisher":"UNICEF Bangladesh","publication_date":"2023-05","url":"https://www.unicef.org/bangladesh/en/reports/public-finance-children","claim":"UNICEF said Bangladesh's flagship Mother and Child Benefit Programme covered 1.25 million beneficiaries in 2022-23 and was planned to expand to 4 million by 2023-24 and 6 million by 2026; it also said the child budget share remained stagnant at 15.05 percent and identified low utilization and rushed end-of-year spending as implementation challenges.","locator":"highlights lines 61-69","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UNICEF policy note with explicit program coverage, financing, and implementation critique.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022-23 social-protection planning and implementation","ruler_attribution":"Indirect government policy under the Hasina administration; the note is not a personal directive by Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["The same note says child-budget shares were stagnant and implementation suffered from low utilization and rushed spending, so it is mixed rather than a clean success story."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Progress on breastfeeding in Bangladesh undermined by aggressive formula milk marketing - WHO, UNICEF","publisher":"UNICEF Bangladesh","publication_date":"2022-02-23","url":"https://www.unicef.org/bangladesh/en/press-releases/progress-breastfeeding-bangladesh-undermined-aggressive-formula-milk-marketing-who","claim":"The WHO/UNICEF report said 51 percent of surveyed parents and pregnant women had been targeted by formula-milk marketing; in Bangladesh, 65 percent of women exclusively breastfed up to five months and 98 percent wanted to breastfeed, yet the report called for stronger enforcement of Bangladesh's national code legislation and breastfeeding support.","locator":"lines 66-85","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary WHO/UNICEF report with Bangladesh-specific data and a clear policy recommendation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"target-year 2022 health and infant-nutrition context","ruler_attribution":"Mostly system-level rather than personal attribution; useful as evidence of regulatory gap under the Hasina administration.","contrary_evidence":["The source shows strong breastfeeding intent among families, so the problem is partly regulatory and enforcement-related rather than simply behavioral."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNICEF: New report details shocking deprivation of children living on the street","publisher":"UNICEF Bangladesh","publication_date":"2023-04-10","url":"https://www.unicef.org/bangladesh/en/press-releases/unicef-new-report-details-shocking-deprivation-children-living-street","claim":"The Survey on Street Children 2022 found that children living on the street in Bangladesh faced deprivation, extreme poverty, malnutrition, disease, illiteracy, and violence; UNICEF said the numbers could be in the millions and that it was working with the government as it invested in more social workers to help children leave the streets.","locator":"lines 67-93","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UNICEF release based on a 2022 survey with specific vulnerability and harm indicators.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"retrospective on 2022 conditions","ruler_attribution":"Indirect government context under the Hasina administration; the source does not isolate one decision-maker but does identify the need for state response.","contrary_evidence":["The report is about severe unmet need, so it is mainly negative evidence even though it also notes government social-worker involvement."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 25-40 documents across UNICEF, WHO, WFP, World Bank, UNDP, the Bangladesh social-protection portal, ministry pages, and several Bangladesh news rewrites. I kept the strongest non-duplicative items that gave either target-year policy intent, implementation detail, or retrospective outcome data.

Retained because they added distinct 2022 or 2022-retrospective evidence:
- UNICEF budget-attitudes survey on education, health, protection, and social workers.
- UNICEF child-protection workforce commitment.
- UNICEF remote-learning strategy for marginalized children.
- WHO country cooperation strategy on health progress and remaining system gaps.
- WFP country strategic plan for 2022-2026 and WFP Bangladesh assessments.
- UNICEF flood-response release.
- World Bank poverty-reduction feature with 2022 outcome data.
- UNICEF Public Finance for Children on MCBP and child-budget implementation.
- WHO/UNICEF breastfeeding report on formula-marketing weakness.
- UNICEF street-children survey release.

Considered but rejected as duplicate, too generic, blocked, or out of period:
- IMF e-library country-report material was blocked by 403, so I relied on accessible UNICEF, WHO, WFP, and World Bank sources instead.
- Broad UNICEF data dashboards and static country profile pages were useful for context but not retained as chapter evidence because they did not add ruler-specific 2022 action.
- Later 2024-2026 SSPS and UNICEF/World Bank pages were not retained unless they clearly reported 2022 conditions retrospectively.
- Government index/listing pages and repository pages without substantive report text were skipped.
- Rewrites of the same flood or budget story across multiple publishers were deduplicated.
- Generic global health or poverty pages that did not add Bangladesh-specific 2022 claims were not kept.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct, ruler-personal evidence is still thin for social welfare. Most evidence is government- or ministry-level, not a clear personal directive from Sheikh Hasina.
- Better evidence would come from 2022 cabinet, PMO, budget-implementation, or audit material tying specific welfare decisions directly to Hasina rather than to ministries, UNICEF, WHO, or WFP partner structures.
- More precise 2022 data on urban poor, regional disparities, disability coverage, and service quality would help with the vulnerable-groups and dignity lenses.
- Stronger implementation evidence on how much of the social-protection and child-protection expansion actually reached intended beneficiaries would improve confidence.
- More microdata on nutrition, school recovery, and household access by wealth quintile would sharpen the baseline-adjusted life-chances assessment.
